{"records":[{"id":"hv4x95xj65","title":"Anti-cancer activity, docking studies and characterization of  indole tethered 1,3,4-oxadiazole hybrids ","description":"This data pertains to the prediction and contribution of substituents in the oxadiazole dataset towards its anticancer potential. A set of oxadiazoles (6a-l) linked with indole were produced by heating pure compounds 5a-l with an excess of acetic anhydride for a duration of 4-5 hours. The resulting compounds were analyzed using FTIR, 1HNMR, 13C NMR, and mass spectroscopy. The article included spectral data of the compounds, as seen in Figure 5-19. The anticancer properties of newly synthesized compounds 6a-l were displayed graphically in Fig.20. The indole and oxadiazole moieties were combined in the proposed scaffold, and additional structural alterations were introduced to investigate the impact of these changes on the anticancer activity against MDA MB 361 cancer cells. The findings are presented in this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-31T14:46:05.315Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hv4x95xj65","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hv4x95xj65"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dr Bharath rathna Kumar","last_name":"Ponniah","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sunil","last_name":"kadiri","orcid":null},{"first_name":"DEEPAK SHAMRAO","last_name":"KHOBRAGADE","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Venu","last_name":"Priya","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sampath A ","last_name":"Gouru","orcid":null},{"first_name":" Srilakshmi:","last_name":"Singagari","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Organic Chemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m4g4dyvvcm","title":"Data for: Austalides V and W,  New Meroterpenoids from the Fungus Aspergillus ustus  and Their Anti-Cancer Activities","description":"This research is dedicated to the isolation and determination of the structure of two new metabolites (austalides V and W) from the austalide meroterpenoids and the study of their anticancer activity. The structures of novel metabolites were established by modern physical methods (multinuclear NMR and mass-spectrometry) and by comparison with related known compounds. The main structural feature of both compounds is a five-membered cycle (G), an unprecedented structural fragment, first found in austalides. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-26T20:23:27.089Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m4g4dyvvcm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m4g4dyvvcm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tatiana","last_name":"Antipova","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Filamentous Fungus","Terpenoids"],"journal_issn":["0960894X"]},{"id":"g5b8ynm842","title":"MALT1 uses K63-linked and linear ubiquitin chains to facilitate recruitment and cleavage of substrates containing ubiquitin binding domains. Long et al.","description":"Quoting directly from the abstract: The protease mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue-1 (MALT1) regulates various aspects of T-and B-cell receptor (T/BCR)-induced lymphocyte activation and development. Outstanding questions surround how MALT1-protease functions, including how substrates are identified, and how to modulate substrate spectrum pharmacologically. Here, using cells deficient for assembling MALT1-protease complex K63-linked- and/or linear-ubiquitin chains during T/BCR signaling or by ablating ubiquitin binding sites of specific MALT1 substrates, we identified a protease recognition paradigm wherein ubiquitin chains on the MALT1-protease complex attract specific ubiquitin-binding substrates. Pharmaceutical suppression of linear/K63-linked ubiquitin chain formation in primary lymphocytes and cell lines boosted or suppressed specific MALT1-dependent substrate cleavage events in a predictable manner based on ubiquitin-binding properties of each substrate. Substrate spectrum modulation altered cytokine response of lymphocytes, dependent on changes in MALT1’s substrate cleavage. Thus, we identify a new paradigm for protease-substrate recognition, distinct from other ubiquitin-mediated protease mechanisms where ubiquitin tags the substrate. This information has significant ramifications for immune regulation and cancer therapy. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:05:39.436Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g5b8ynm842","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g5b8ynm842"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"marcus","last_name":"Long","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manhui","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Margot","last_name":"Thome","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/053gv2m95","name":"Novartis Institutes for BioMedical Research","grant_id":"NA"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01pd7my79","name":"Swiss Cancer League","grant_id":"NA"}],"categories":["Immunity","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vpps4vhryg","title":"COVID-19 related trajectory data of 10 microseconds all atom molecular dynamics simulation of SARS-CoV-2 dimeric main protease","description":"Raw trajectory data (GROMACS format) of 10 microseconds all atom molecular dynamics simulation of COVID-19 related SARS-CoV-2 dimeric main protease without ligand based on PDB 6LU7, were caclulated on a special purpose computer, MDGRAPE-4A, at RIKEN BDR, JAPAN. We can observe relaxation dynamics and fluctuations of the dimeric protease over the microseconds timescale. We hope that this raw data can help drug development efforts targeting the SARS-CoV-2 main protease. Preparation details and summaries will be submitted to \"Data in Brief\". More data might be added as new version or another dataset.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-27T07:06:25.625Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vpps4vhryg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vpps4vhryg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Teruhisa S.","last_name":"KOMATSU","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yohei M.","last_name":"KOYAMA","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Noriaki","last_name":"OKIMOTO","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gentaro","last_name":"MORIMOTO","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yousuke","last_name":"OHNO","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Makoto","last_name":"TAIJI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Drug","Molecular Dynamics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"98s3brhnyf","title":"Terpenoids from Daphne pedunculata and their cytotoxic activities in vitro","description":"This dataset is the research data of the article titled \"Terpenoids from Daphne pedunculata and Their Cytotoxic Activities In Vitro\". The highlights of the research are:\n1. Three new terpenoids, including two tigliane-type diterpenoids and one daucane sesquiterpenoid, were isolated from Daphne pedunculata H. F. Zhou ex C. Y. Chang.\n2. Compounds 2, 5, and 8 showed moderate cytotoxic activities against A549, MDA-MB-231, and SW480 cancer cell lines.\n3. Compound 2 inhibited A549 cell growth in a concentration-dependent manner, indicating its potential as an anticancer agent.used for supplemtary","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T16:53:48.444Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/98s3brhnyf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/98s3brhnyf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lin","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xz7f3wbzrc","title":"PPARγ/ESCRT – FLSs exosomal ANXA1 – ERK axis Alleviates Chondrocyte Osteoarthritis in Exercise therapy","description":" Exercise induced PPARγ controls the ESCRT-dependent pathway to activate exosome biogenesis, the exosomal ANXA1 act on chondrocyte to interact with ERK then activate autophagy and ameliorate cartilage degeneration. PPARγ/ESCRT – FLSs exosomal ANXA1 – ERK axis provides a deeper theoretical basis for exercise therapy of OA and a new idea for the clinical transformation of nanoparticles into OA therapy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-28T16:36:58.882Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xz7f3wbzrc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xz7f3wbzrc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shuangshuo","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Osteoarthritis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tpcdgw7h6m","title":"Activating alternative transport modes in a multidrug resistance efflux pump to confer chemical susceptibility","description":"Small multidrug resistance (SMR) transporters perform coupled antiport of protons and toxic substrates, contributing to antibiotic resistance through efflux of these compounds from the bacterial cytoplasm. Extensive biophysical studies of the molecular transport mechanism of the E. coli SMR transporter EmrE indicate that it should also be capable of performing proton/drug symport or uniport, either of which will lead to drug susceptibility rather than drug resistance in vivo. Here we show that EmrE does indeed confer susceptibility to some small molecule substrates in the native E. coli in addition to conferring resistance to known polyaromatic cation substrates. In vitro experiments show that substrate binding at a secondary site triggers uncoupled proton uniport that leads to susceptibility. These results suggest that the SMR transporters provide one avenue for bacterial-selective dissipation of the proton-motive force. This has potential for antibiotic development and disruption of antibiotic resistance due to drug efflux more broadly.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-20T21:12:18.622Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tpcdgw7h6m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tpcdgw7h6m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peyton","last_name":"Spreacker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathan","last_name":"Thomas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Will","last_name":"Beeninga","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Merissa","last_name":"Brousseau","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Colin","last_name":"Porter","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kylie","last_name":"Hibbs","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine","last_name":"Henzler-Wildman","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Electrophysiology","Multiple Drug Resistance","Microarray","Escherichia coli"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nrhwvdx79t","title":"Plant-derived antiviral compounds ","description":"\nThis datasets currently provide the chemical compounds extracted from Bangladeshi originated plant species. This datasets currently targeted against Covid-19 to find out novel therapeutic candidates from local plant species. The compounds were enlisted upon having reported in plant extracts and literature-based survey data. Currently, about 119 plant species have taken into consideration and we are working inexorably to expand the datasets. About 1480 compounds were retrieved from Pub Chem database as an SDF format to attract a wider group for bioinformatics applications including; structure-based drug designing, and scaffold hopping, lead identification for therapeutic targets.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-10T20:35:35.085Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nrhwvdx79t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nrhwvdx79t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Md.Rimon","last_name":"Parves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shafi","last_name":"Mahmud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raihan ","last_name":"Uddin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Khaled Mahmud","last_name":"Sujon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ekhtiar","last_name":"Rahman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mobassir Noor ","last_name":"Shehab","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Md. Wasim ","last_name":"Alom","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Al","last_name":"Amin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Al Shahriar ","last_name":"Akash","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ariful","last_name":"Islam","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Phytochemicals","Pharmaceutical Chemistry","Computational Medicinal Chemistry","Phytochemistry","Phytopharmacy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s72rcpk82b","title":"Supplementary Dataset of Betel Plant GC-MS and Docking Results","description":"This dataset contains information about the GC-MS results from the betel plant along with the molecular docking results of the compounds detected using GC-MS against the main protease from SARS-CoV-2. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-19T14:21:57.654Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s72rcpk82b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s72rcpk82b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fatimawali","last_name":"Fatimawali","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Trina Ekawati","last_name":"Tallei","orcid":"0000-0002-7963-7527"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry","Drug Discovery","In Silico Study","Molecular Docking"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"smrx2z8tdn","title":"Synthesis and Functional Proteomics Evaluation of Quinazolin-4-One Derivatives as Proteasome Inhibitors_Proteomics data_Part 6","description":"Quinazolinones have been recently recognized as preferred scaffolds for developing novel therapeutic opportunities: they indeed exhibit a wide range of biological activities, including antifungal, antitubercular, antihypertensive, anticancer, and antiviral ones. In this work, a focused library of new bioactive 4-(3-H)-quinazolinones has been synthesized, their cytotoxic action against DU-145 prostate cancer cells has been detailed and compound 4k has been revealed as the most active one. Consequently, its interactome has been characterized, using a label-free functional proteomics-based platform coupling Drug Affinity Responsive Target Stability (DARTS) and targeted Limited Proteolysis-Multiple Reaction Monitoring-Mass Spectrometry (t-LiP-MRM-MS). This multi-faced strategy has been employed to reveal few subunits of the 26S proteasome machinery as the most reliable compound 4k biological targets. This paved the way for the deepening of the protein–ligand interaction using in-vitro and in-silico bio-orthogonal techniques. Finally, the analysis of its function in living DU-145 cells prompted compound 4k as a new leading inhibitor of the chymotrypsin-like activity of the proteasomal β-5 subunit, stirring the quinazolinone framework for the development of new anticancer drugs. \nThis data set contains the following DARTS proteomics data: exp3, part 2\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-09T14:30:24.016Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/smrx2z8tdn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/smrx2z8tdn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elva","last_name":"Morretta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria Chiara","last_name":"Monti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Proteomics","Target Identification"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142853,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":186,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142853,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142057,"restricted":73,"embargoed":723},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2348},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":713},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":482},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":450},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":337},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":283},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":237}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140180},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1916},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":184},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":80},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":44},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":538},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":410},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":388},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":328}]}}